  #block(breakable: false)[
  == Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Scarcity against unlimited wants is the definition of the economic problem; the other options are consequences or applications of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Capital — goods used to produce other goods. Money and its paper claims are means of acquiring factors, not factors themselves.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Profit, and with it the risk of loss. Wages go to labour, rent to land and interest to capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The economy can produce more of both goods than before — the graphical signature of growth.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The economy could produce more of both goods, so resources are idle or inefficiently used.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The alternative given up. Money cost is a price, and the other two are accounting rather than economic costs.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The new house is output produced this year. The used car was counted when it was built, a pension is a transfer, and buying shares merely exchanges one asset for another.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A pension moves income between people; nothing new is produced, so counting it would double count.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Their value is already inside the final good; counting them again would inflate GDP by the number of stages of production.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Government and the foreign sector add injections and leakages to the simple two-sector model.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    S + T + M = I + G + X. Each of the other equalities is a single pair within that balance, not the balance itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It strips the price change out, leaving the change in real output.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    ₦120 ÷ 1.50 = ₦80 billion. Multiplying by the index instead of dividing gives ₦180 billion, which is the predictable error.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Two people with the same average income can have very different distributions, and housework and subsistence output appear in no account.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Taxation takes purchasing power out of the domestic flow; the other three are injections into it.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The multiplier is 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5, so 5 × ₦2 billion = ₦10 billion — the whole of the successive rounds of spending, not just the first.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A higher marginal propensity to consume leaves less of each round leaking out, so the successive rounds are larger.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Net factor income is positive, so adding it to GDP gives a larger GNP — the position of a country with substantial overseas investment.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The capital worn out during the year must be replaced before the economy's income can be counted as net.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It removes both the price change and the population change, so what is left is output per person at constant prices.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    General acceptability is the defining property; notes and coins are only one form of it and wealth is a much wider category.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A common measure of value allows prices to be compared and accounts to be kept.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Portability is about carriage; durability is about wear, divisibility about small purchases and difficulty of imitation about forgery.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Money held loses purchasing power, so people hold less of it and buy goods instead — the flight from money.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Notes, coins and, above all, deposits. Gold and reserves back the currency but are not themselves the money supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The reserve ratio sets the arithmetic limit and the demand for loans sets the practical one; neither branch numbers nor exports enters it.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Deposits are owed to customers. Loans, securities and buildings are the assets that back them.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It is a reserve requirement against deposits, which is why raising it reduces the funds available for lending.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Keeping the accounts and handling borrowing are the banking services; the other options are separate functions.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Buying securities adds reserves and selling them drains reserves, and reserves determine how much credit the banks can create.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Borrowing becomes dearer, so credit falls and investment with it. The effect on exports would come only indirectly through the exchange rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Different institutions serve different functions, which is what the term describes.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Long-term project finance rather than short-term commercial lending — the gap that commercial banks normally leave.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Loans and advances earn the income and form the largest asset; deposits are on the other side of the balance sheet.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Stagflation — stagnation plus inflation. It defeats the standard remedies, since fighting inflation deepens the slump and fighting the slump feeds the inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Both instruments reduce aggregate demand, which is what is pulling prices up.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Restraining demand would raise unemployment without touching the cost pressure, so prices and incomes policy or supply-side measures are needed.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A fixed money debt is repaid in money that is worth more, so the real burden rises — the reason central banks fear deflation as much as inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A fixed money income buys less and a fixed money claim is repaid in cheaper currency, so both lose while debtors gain.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Lower unemployment is associated with higher inflation along the curve, though the curve shifts when expectations change.
  ]

  #sol-item(41, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It is compulsory and general; a voluntary payment is a donation, a service charge is a price, and a loan must be repaid.
  ]

  #sol-item(42, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It looks forward, setting out what the government expects to receive and intends to spend.
  ]

  #sol-item(43, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Borrowing at home or abroad, or using accumulated reserves; the other options are monetary actions, not sources of funds.
  ]

  #sol-item(44, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The exchange rate is set by the monetary authority, not by the budget. The other three are standard fiscal objectives.
  ]

  #sol-item(45, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Income tax is levied on the person's income and cannot be shifted; the other three are indirect taxes on transactions.
  ]

  #sol-item(46, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The rich pay a larger proportion, which narrows the gap between incomes.
  ]

  #sol-item(47, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Buyers cannot reduce quantity, so they pay the whole tax — the extreme case of the incidence rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(48, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Producers cannot pass it on to buyers who can easily walk away, so they absorb it.
  ]

  #sol-item(49, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Revenue rises with the rate up to a point and then falls, because high rates discourage the activity being taxed.
  ]

  #sol-item(50, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Beyond the peak the tax base shrinks faster than the rate rises, so revenue falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(51, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The same rate on a basic good takes a bigger proportion of a small income, which is regression in effect.
  ]

  #sol-item(52, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Spending that builds a lasting asset — here human capital — is capital expenditure, even though much of the salary bill inside it recurs annually.
  ]

  #sol-item(53, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Payments for which no good or service is currently produced in return; salaries and purchases buy services and goods, and interest is a payment for the use of capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(54, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Contraction: higher taxes and lower spending, which is the mirror image of the recession policy.
  ]

  #sol-item(55, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Progressive taxes and unemployment benefits move automatically with the cycle, damping both booms and recessions.
  ]

  #sol-item(56, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It is domestic borrowing, so repayment stays within the economy, though it still transfers income to bondholders.
  ]

  #sol-item(57, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    When servicing crowds out spending on services and infrastructure, the debt has become a fiscal constraint.
  ]

  #sol-item(58, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The spending enters the flow while the economy's capacity has not grown, so prices are bid up.
  ]

  #sol-item(59, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Powers and duties are allocated across federal, state and local government, with revenue shared through the Federation Account.
  ]

  #sol-item(60, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Ability to pay is the equity criterion; ease of collection is administrative efficiency and maximum revenue is yield.
  ]

  #sol-item(61, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Currency, law, language and distance all cross a border; the gains from specialisation are the same in both.
  ]

  #sol-item(62, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Fewer inputs per unit. Comparative advantage, by contrast, is about the opportunity cost of the alternative given up.
  ]

  #sol-item(63, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The opportunity-cost comparison is the definition; total output and money prices are absolute, not comparative, measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(64, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Specialise where the opportunity cost is lowest and exchange — both partners can end up with more than in autarky.
  ]

  #sol-item(65, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The ratio has moved in the country's favour; a rise in export volume at unchanged prices does not change the terms of trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(66, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A fixed sum per unit. A percentage of value is an ad valorem tariff, a quantity limit is a quota, and a payment to exporters is a subsidy.
  ]

  #sol-item(67, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The price rises for domestic buyers, which is exactly why producers want protection and consumers lose.
  ]

  #sol-item(68, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The scarcity rent from the licence goes to whoever holds it, whereas tariff revenue goes to the treasury by definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(69, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Shelter from competition removes the pressure to cut costs, so the protection tends to become permanent.
  ]

  #sol-item(70, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    That is precisely the relative-price change intended, which is why the elasticity of demand determines whether the trade balance improves.
  ]

  #sol-item(71, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Contracts are fixed in the short run, so the trade balance worsens before the quantity response arrives.
  ]

  #sol-item(72, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The accounts must balance, so a current-account surplus shows up as reserve accumulation or capital export.
  ]

  #sol-item(73, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Capital inflow finances the current-account deficit; the balance of payments identity requires it.
  ]

  #sol-item(74, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Visible trade, invisible trade and transfers such as remittances form the current account; loans and portfolio flows are capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(75, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    They are unilateral transfers — income sent home with nothing supplied in return — so they sit in the current account.
  ]

  #sol-item(76, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Member states adopt one tariff against outsiders; free movement of labour and a single currency go beyond a customs union.
  ]

  #sol-item(77, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Genuine efficiency gain; the second option is trade diversion, its opposite.
  ]

  #sol-item(78, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Integration through trade liberalisation and cooperation, with a single currency still a long-standing aspiration rather than a fact.
  ]

  #sol-item(79, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A continental free trade area, progressively removing tariffs on intra-African trade.
  ]

  #sol-item(80, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Price-taking in a world market is the vulnerability; domestic costs affect the volume little in the short run.
  ]

  #sol-item(81, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Real output over time. Welfare, employment and prices can move with growth but are not the definition of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(82, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Health is a welfare indicator, which development includes and growth does not measure.
  ]

  #sol-item(83, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Health and knowledge — three components combined as a geometric mean.
  ]

  #sol-item(84, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The non-income components have deteriorated enough to outweigh the income gain — growth without development.
  ]

  #sol-item(85, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Zero is perfect equality and one is complete inequality; the conventional measure is scaled to the unit interval.
  ]

  #sol-item(86, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The distribution has become more unequal, whichever direction average income moved.
  ]

  #sol-item(87, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A poverty line fixed in real terms; relative poverty uses the society's own average as the reference.
  ]

  #sol-item(88, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    More workers per dependant, which is the demographic dividend — and a temporary window, since the workers will eventually age.
  ]

  #sol-item(89, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Ages on one axis, numbers on the other, split by sex — a picture of structure rather than of totals.
  ]

  #sol-item(90, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Many children at the base, which means both high dependency now and rapid labour-force growth later.
  ]

  #sol-item(91, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    More people sharing slower-growing output — the arithmetic of the population trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(92, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Neither students nor retirees are available for work or seeking it; the unemployed who are seeking work are inside the definition by contrast.
  ]

  #sol-item(93, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
